{
  "summary": "While the spec review idea has merit in theory, it has several weaknesses worth examining. First, it risks redundancy: the existing review step (review_impl) already catches spec-level problems — if the implementation doesn't match the plan or has gaps, that surfaces during code review and triggers a retry loop back to implementing. Adding spec review means errors could be caught at two different gates with overlapping criteria, adding latency without proportional value. Second, the proposal assumes spec quality is a real bottleneck, but the current pipeline already has a plan review step that validates the high-level approach, and the spec is largely a mechanical translation of an approved plan into implementation instructions — the failure mode isn't 'bad spec' so much as 'bad implementation of a decent spec.' Third, the retry logic adds complexity: if a spec fails review and goes back to spec-writing, the spec-writer may produce a similar output since it's working from the same approved plan, creating a loop without meaningful progress. A lighter-weight alternative worth considering would be to expand the existing plan review to also validate that the plan is specific enough to produce good specs, or to add spec-quality checks as part of the implementation review rather than as a separate pipeline stage."
}
